const { createClient } = require('@supabase/supabase-js');
const supabase = createClient('https://vgujnkxylipfwmkpwzvb.supabase.co', process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY);

const transcripts = {
  '29a1da8d-2525-4065-bbcb-b26f955b53b0': `Welcome to the Project Management Professional course, Lesson 1.1 — What is Project Management? I want to start with a definition that goes beyond the textbook. Project management is the discipline of turning uncertainty into predictability, and chaos into controlled, value-delivering work. Every project starts with a goal, a deadline, and a budget — and the space between where you are and where you need to be is filled with complexity, competing priorities, and unexpected obstacles. The project manager's job is to navigate that space successfully. The PMI defines a project as a temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product, service, or result. Three words matter here. Temporary — it has a defined beginning and end. Unique — it is not routine operational work. Result — it delivers something of value. This distinguishes projects from operations, which are ongoing and repetitive. Project management as a formal discipline emerged from the engineering and construction sectors in the mid-twentieth century. The Manhattan Project, the Apollo program, and large infrastructure projects demonstrated that complex, multi-stakeholder endeavors required systematic planning, coordination, and control. The tools developed for these mega-projects — work breakdown structures, critical path analysis, earned value management — became the foundation of modern project management methodology. Today, project management spans every industry and every type of organization. Healthcare, finance, technology, government, nonprofits — all use project management to deliver strategic initiatives. The Project Management Professional certification, the PMP, is recognized as the gold standard for project management competency globally. Earning it demonstrates not just knowledge of methodologies but the practical ability to apply them across different contexts and challenges. This course will prepare you for that certification while building the practical skills you will use every day as a project leader.`,

  '6a6461b9-8f5b-40bc-9292-180fedcf3a6f': `Lesson 1.2 — Project Lifecycle and Process Groups. Understanding the project lifecycle is fundamental to everything else in project management. It gives you a mental model for where you are in a project, what work is appropriate at each stage, and what outputs you need to produce before moving forward. The project lifecycle typically moves through four phases: Initiating, Planning, Executing, and Closing. Some methodologies add a Monitoring and Controlling phase as a parallel activity that runs throughout. During Initiating, the project is formally authorized. The project charter is created, key stakeholders are identified, and the project manager is assigned. This phase answers the question: should we do this project and why? During Planning, the project scope, schedule, budget, quality standards, communication plan, risk register, and resource plan are all developed and baselined. Planning is where the project manager earns their value — the quality of the plan directly determines the probability of successful execution. The common mistake is rushing through planning to get to execution. This virtually always results in more time lost to rework and re-planning during execution than was saved by shortening the planning phase. During Executing, the project plan is put into action. Work packages are assigned, resources are coordinated, procurements are managed, and stakeholders are kept engaged and informed. The project manager's primary role shifts from planner to communicator, facilitator, and obstacle remover. During Closing, the project is formally completed. Deliverables are accepted, contracts are closed, lessons learned are documented, and the project team is released. Many project managers underinvest in closing, which means lessons learned are lost and the organization does not capture the institutional knowledge the project generated. The five PMI Process Groups — Initiating, Planning, Executing, Monitoring and Controlling, and Closing — map to this lifecycle and provide the structured framework for the PMP exam.`,

  'a7b40f28-857b-4dd6-a82f-fc195ddd904a': `Lesson 1.3 — Organizational Structures and PM Authority. One of the most significant determinants of a project manager's effectiveness is something most new PMs do not think about: the organizational structure they are working within. Your authority, your resource access, your ability to make decisions — all of these are profoundly shaped by how your organization is structured. Let me walk you through the three primary organizational structures and what they mean for you as a PM. In a Functional Organization, work is organized by department — engineering, marketing, finance, operations. Project managers in functional organizations have the weakest authority. Team members report to their functional manager, not to the PM. Budget control sits with the functional manager. The PM essentially coordinates work rather than directing it. Getting resources for your project requires negotiation with functional managers who have their own competing priorities. Functional structures work reasonably well for projects that are primarily contained within a single department, but they create significant coordination challenges for cross-functional projects. In a Projectized Organization, the PM is king. Teams are organized around projects, the PM controls the budget and resources, and team members report directly to the PM for the duration of the project. This structure gives PMs maximum authority and control, but it can create inefficiency because specialized expertise is duplicated across project teams rather than shared centrally. In a Matrix Organization, the most common structure in modern enterprises, team members have two reporting relationships: to their functional manager and to the project manager. The balance of authority between these two managers varies along a spectrum from weak matrix, where functional managers dominate, to strong matrix, where PMs have significant authority, to balanced matrix, where authority is roughly shared. Understanding where your organization falls on this spectrum is essential for calibrating your influence strategies.`,

  'a7ccfea9-90b9-4450-867d-cddd45f178f7': `Lesson 1.4 — The Project Charter and Business Case. The project charter is the document that formally authorizes a project and gives the project manager the authority to apply organizational resources to project activities. It is the foundation document from which everything else flows, and getting it right is one of the most important things you will do on any project. Let me walk you through what a strong project charter contains and why each element matters. The purpose and justification section explains why this project exists — what business problem it solves, what opportunity it captures, or what strategic objective it advances. This section is critically important because it establishes the context for every scope decision you will make throughout the project. When stakeholders argue for scope additions, the purpose section is your reference point: does this addition advance the stated purpose? If not, it does not belong in this project. The high-level scope section defines what is in the project and what is explicitly out of scope. Listing out-of-scope items is as important as listing in-scope work — it prevents scope creep by forcing early decisions about boundaries. The deliverables section specifies what the project will produce — the tangible outputs that define completion. Deliverables should be specific enough to verify completion but not so detailed that they constrain the execution approach. The project constraints section documents the triple constraint: scope, schedule, and budget. More specifically, it establishes which constraint is fixed and which are flexible. Is the deadline immovable? Is the budget a hard cap? Is the scope fully defined or negotiable? Understanding the constraint priorities is essential for making good trade-off decisions throughout execution. The project sponsor signature on the charter is not ceremonial — it represents formal organizational commitment to the project, to the PM's authority, and to the resource investments required to deliver it.`,

  '163392aa-66fe-49d7-ae29-466280bb7134': `Lesson 1.5 — Stakeholder Identification and the Stakeholder Register. Every project succeeds or fails in large part based on how well the project manager understands and manages the stakeholder landscape. Stakeholders are individuals, groups, or organizations who may affect, be affected by, or perceive themselves to be affected by a project decision, activity, or outcome. That is a broad definition, and it is intentionally broad — the biggest stakeholder management mistakes happen when PMs define the stakeholder population too narrowly. Stakeholder identification is not a one-time activity at project initiation. It is an ongoing discipline throughout the project lifecycle. New stakeholders emerge as the project progresses — regulatory bodies become relevant when you reach certain milestones, end users become engaged during testing, executive sponsors' interest increases as you approach delivery. The stakeholder register is the primary tool for capturing and managing stakeholder information. For each stakeholder, the register should capture their role and organizational relationship to the project, their interest in the project — what they want it to achieve or avoid, their power or influence over project decisions and outcomes, their current attitude — supportive, neutral, or resistant — and their information needs and preferred communication approach. The power-interest grid is a useful analysis tool for prioritizing your stakeholder engagement efforts. Stakeholders with high power and high interest — your executive sponsor, your primary customer — require close management and frequent communication. Stakeholders with high power but low interest need to be kept satisfied — they can derail the project if they become unhappy, even though they are not closely engaged in day-to-day work. Low power, high interest stakeholders should be kept informed. Low power, low interest stakeholders can be monitored with minimal effort. The goal of stakeholder management is not to make everyone happy — it is to maintain the support necessary for project success while managing resistance appropriately.`,

  // MODULE 2 - SCOPE
  '13375ebd-8459-4534-8dc0-8131153850a7': `Welcome to Module 2, Lesson 2.1 — Collecting Requirements. Requirements collection is the process of determining, documenting, and managing stakeholder needs and requirements to meet project objectives. It sounds straightforward, but in practice it is one of the most technically challenging and interpersonally complex activities in project management. Let me explain why and how to do it well. The fundamental challenge of requirements collection is that stakeholders often do not know what they want until they see what they do not want. They describe requirements in terms of their current problems rather than their future needs. They assume context that they have never explicitly communicated. And they frequently disagree with each other about what the project should deliver. Your job is to navigate all of this complexity and produce a requirements baseline that is complete, consistent, verifiable, and acceptable to the key stakeholders. The primary elicitation techniques available to you are interviews, which work well for individual stakeholders with specialized knowledge or unique perspectives; focus groups, which bring together stakeholders with similar interests to discuss requirements collectively; workshops, which are structured group sessions that efficiently elicit and reconcile requirements from multiple stakeholders simultaneously — this is the most time-efficient technique when the stakeholder group is manageable in size; surveys and questionnaires, which are effective for large stakeholder populations where individual interviews are impractical; observation, which involves watching how users actually do their work rather than relying on their description of it — users often cannot accurately describe their own workflows because so much of their work is habitual and unconscious; and prototypes, which give stakeholders something tangible to react to rather than asking them to imagine an abstract future system. Document all requirements in a requirements traceability matrix that links each requirement to its business objective, its source stakeholder, and eventually to the work package that delivers it.`,

  '80df2ba3-e6f8-46b0-a157-b6999ea18258': `Lesson 2.2 — Defining Scope and the Work Breakdown Structure. Once requirements are collected and approved, the project manager must translate them into a defined project scope — a clear, documented description of what the project will and will not deliver. The scope statement is your most important planning document because everything else — the schedule, the budget, the resource plan, the risk register — is derived from it. A well-written scope statement has three components: product scope, which describes the characteristics and features of the deliverable; project scope, which describes the work required to deliver the product scope; and explicit scope exclusions, which document what is deliberately not included to prevent scope creep. The Work Breakdown Structure, or WBS, is the decomposition of the total scope of project work into smaller, more manageable components. Think of it as a hierarchical map of everything the project will produce. The highest level represents the project itself. The next level represents major deliverables or project phases. Each subsequent level decomposes those deliverables into progressively smaller work packages until you reach a level that can be reliably estimated, assigned, and tracked. The WBS is the foundation of the project schedule and budget. You cannot build a credible schedule without a complete WBS because you cannot estimate time and resources for work you have not defined. The most common WBS mistake is stopping decomposition too early, leaving large, ambiguous work packages that are difficult to estimate and track. The WBS dictionary is a companion document that provides detailed descriptions of each WBS element — the work involved, the resources required, the acceptance criteria, and the estimated costs. Together, the WBS and WBS dictionary constitute the scope baseline against which project performance is measured.`,

  '3e170e36-4f2e-4eff-92aa-49cc09234050': `Lesson 2.3 — Scope Validation and Control. Scope validation is the process of formalizing acceptance of completed project deliverables. It is distinct from quality control, which verifies that deliverables meet quality standards. Scope validation verifies that deliverables meet the requirements as defined in the scope baseline. The key output of scope validation is formal acceptance — documented confirmation from the appropriate stakeholder that a deliverable meets the agreed requirements and is accepted. This acceptance is your protection against later claims that the deliverable was not what was needed. Scope control is the process of monitoring the status of the project scope and managing changes to the scope baseline. Scope creep — the gradual expansion of scope without corresponding adjustments to time, cost, or resources — is one of the leading causes of project failure. It happens in two ways: unauthorized scope additions, where team members implement features that were not approved; and approved scope additions that are not accompanied by timeline and budget adjustments. Your change control process is your primary defense against scope creep. Every potential scope change should be formally evaluated for impact on schedule, cost, and resources before being approved or rejected. The discipline of running every scope change through this process — even when it feels bureaucratic — protects the project's triple constraint integrity. When stakeholders try to bypass the change control process with phrases like this is a small thing that will only take a few hours, your response is clear: all scope changes go through the process, no matter how small. The reason is not bureaucracy — it is that scope creep typically happens one small thing at a time, each individually insignificant but collectively fatal to the project.`,

  '9f62997f-d0a1-425c-9b09-5f971a95f3a5': `Lesson 2.4 — Agile Scope Management and the Product Backlog. Traditional project management treats scope as something to be defined upfront and protected from change. Agile project management treats scope as something to be discovered and refined iteratively as the team learns more about what the customer actually needs. Both approaches are valid — the key is choosing the right approach for your context. In Agile scope management, the product backlog is the primary scope artifact. It is a prioritized list of everything the product needs — features, bug fixes, technical improvements, and knowledge acquisition. Unlike a WBS, which is created upfront and kept stable, the product backlog is a living document that is continuously refined as the team learns more. Product backlog management has three key activities. Backlog creation involves capturing all known requirements as user stories or other backlog item formats. The initial backlog is deliberately incomplete — it captures what is known now and expects that more will be discovered through development. Backlog refinement is the ongoing process of adding detail, estimates, and order to backlog items. Typically 10 percent of the team's capacity is dedicated to refinement in each sprint. Backlog prioritization determines the order in which items will be addressed. In Scrum, the Product Owner is solely responsible for prioritization, using a combination of business value, risk reduction, and strategic alignment as prioritization criteria. For project managers working in hybrid environments — where some work is traditional and some is agile — the challenge is integrating backlog-driven agile work with fixed-scope, fixed-deadline traditional project commitments. The most successful approach treats the agile components as a separate work stream with their own backlog and velocity metrics, and integrates their outputs into the overall project timeline through carefully managed integration points.`,

  '2aabcb64-2bcc-4cf0-8cae-316eb61fa0cd': `Lesson 2.5 — AI Tools for Scope Management. Artificial intelligence is transforming scope management in ways that directly address its traditional weaknesses: requirements completeness, scope change impact analysis, and scope creep detection. Let me walk you through the specific AI applications that are most valuable for project managers. AI-powered requirements analysis takes a set of requirements documents and identifies gaps, ambiguities, and conflicts that human reviewers frequently miss. Tools like IBM DOORS with AI enhancement can analyze requirements for completeness against a set of quality criteria, flag requirements that are unclear or contradictory, and suggest missing requirements based on patterns from similar projects. This automated analysis typically catches 20 to 30 percent more requirements issues than manual review alone. Scope change impact analysis has historically been a time-consuming manual process: identifying all project elements affected by a proposed scope change and estimating the impact on schedule, cost, and resources. AI tools can automate much of this analysis by mapping the change against the WBS and automatically identifying affected work packages and their interdependencies. What previously took a senior PM several hours now takes minutes, enabling faster decision-making on change requests. Scope creep detection uses AI to analyze project communication patterns — emails, meeting notes, task updates — and flag language that suggests unauthorized scope additions. When a developer's task description includes work that does not trace back to an approved requirement, the AI flags it for the PM's review. This real-time monitoring catches scope creep as it happens rather than after it has already affected the budget. Together, these AI capabilities significantly reduce the administrative burden of scope management while improving the quality and completeness of scope documentation.`,

  // MODULE 3 - SCHEDULE
  'bd0c9282-ad76-423f-b9c1-095f942a315c': `Welcome to Module 3, Lesson 3.1 — Activity Definition and Sequencing. Schedule management begins with the decomposition of work packages from the WBS into individual activities — the specific actions required to produce the work package deliverables. This decomposition is where the schedule plan gets its granularity and actionability. An activity is different from a work package. A work package describes a deliverable; an activity describes the specific work required to produce it. For example, a work package might be User Authentication Module. The activities within it might include Design authentication flow, Implement login API, Implement password reset functionality, Write unit tests, Conduct security review, and Document API endpoints. Each activity should be small enough to estimate reliably, assign to a specific resource, and track to completion. The rule of thumb is that activities should be completable within one to two reporting periods — if your project reports weekly, activities should take no more than one to two weeks. Activity sequencing determines the order in which activities must be performed based on their logical dependencies. The four types of dependencies are finish-to-start, where activity B cannot start until activity A is finished — the most common type; start-to-start, where activity B cannot start until activity A has started; finish-to-finish, where activity B cannot finish until activity A has finished; and start-to-finish, the rarest type, where activity B cannot finish until activity A has started. The Network Diagram visually represents these dependencies and becomes the foundation for critical path analysis. Sequence dependencies can be mandatory — inherent in the nature of the work, like concrete must cure before you can build on it — or discretionary, based on best practices or team preferences. Discretionary dependencies offer schedule flexibility and are the first place to look when you need to compress the schedule.`,

  '1c219459-e290-4d88-9e2c-a54a8bc8625c': `Lesson 3.2 — Critical Path Method. The Critical Path Method, or CPM, is one of the most powerful tools in project management and one that every PMP candidate must understand deeply. The critical path is the longest sequence of dependent activities through the project network, and it determines the minimum project duration. Any delay to an activity on the critical path delays the project end date by exactly the same amount. Activities not on the critical path have float — the amount of time they can be delayed without delaying the project. Understanding and managing the critical path is how project managers actively control project schedule. Let me walk you through the mechanics. To identify the critical path, you perform a forward pass and a backward pass through the network diagram. The forward pass calculates the earliest start and earliest finish for each activity, moving from left to right through the network. The backward pass calculates the latest start and latest finish, moving from right to left. Total float is calculated as: latest start minus earliest start. Activities with zero float are on the critical path. The practical implications for project managers are significant. Critical path activities require your closest attention and most active risk management because any slip directly impacts the project end date. Resources on critical path activities should not be diverted to non-critical work. When you need to compress the schedule — a common stakeholder request — you have two options: crashing, which adds resources to critical path activities to reduce their duration; and fast-tracking, which executes critical path activities in parallel rather than sequentially. Both techniques increase project risk and cost, which is why schedule compression should always be a deliberate decision with explicit stakeholder acceptance of the associated trade-offs.`,

  '93554845-521f-4e78-9ec2-e74969bfb6d5': `Lesson 3.3 — Resource Estimation and Schedule Baseline. Resource estimation answers the question: how much of each type of resource — people, equipment, materials — is needed to complete each activity, and when? This is where schedule planning intersects with resource planning, and where many project schedules fall apart because they are built without realistic resource availability assumptions. There are three primary estimation techniques for determining activity durations. Analogous estimation uses historical data from similar projects or activities to estimate the current project. It is fast and appropriate when detailed information is not available, but its accuracy depends on how similar the reference project actually was to the current one. Parametric estimation uses statistical relationships between historical data and other variables to calculate estimates. For example, if your organization knows that user interface development averages 4 hours per screen based on historical data, and your project requires 20 screens, parametric estimation gives you 80 hours. This is more accurate than analogous estimation when the parameter relationship is stable and the data is reliable. Three-point estimation acknowledges that estimates are inherently uncertain and uses three scenarios — optimistic, most likely, and pessimistic — to calculate a weighted average duration. The PERT formula uses the weighted average of optimistic plus four times most likely plus pessimistic, divided by six. This technique is valuable for activities with high uncertainty and is required knowledge for the PMP exam. The schedule baseline is the approved version of the schedule that becomes the reference point for measuring schedule performance throughout execution. Calculating schedule variance and schedule performance index in earned value analysis requires a stable schedule baseline. Protecting the baseline from unauthorized changes is one of the most important schedule control disciplines.`,

  '7b26d5ce-921d-440e-bad7-3d774258c5ab': `Lesson 3.4 — Schedule Monitoring and Earned Value. Monitoring schedule performance accurately is one of the most important ongoing responsibilities of a project manager. The question that stakeholders always ask — are we on track? — requires more than a progress percentage. It requires a systematic method for measuring performance and forecasting completion. Earned Value Management, or EVM, provides exactly this. EVM integrates scope, schedule, and cost measurements to provide an objective picture of project performance. The three key EVM values are Planned Value, or PV, which is the authorized budget for the work scheduled to be completed by the measurement date; Earned Value, or EV, which is the authorized budget for the work actually completed by the measurement date; and Actual Cost, or AC, which is the actual cost incurred for work performed by the measurement date. From these three values, you calculate the key EVM indicators. Schedule Variance equals EV minus PV. A positive SV means you are ahead of schedule; negative means behind. Schedule Performance Index equals EV divided by PV. An SPI greater than one means you are getting more work done than planned per unit of time; less than one means you are behind. Cost Variance equals EV minus AC. Cost Performance Index equals EV divided by AC. The Estimate at Completion, or EAC, forecasts the total project cost based on current performance. The simplest EAC formula is Budget at Completion divided by CPI, which assumes current cost performance will continue. EVM requires discipline to implement — it requires a detailed WBS, a time-phased budget, and consistent measurement of actual progress. But the investment pays back in the quality of management information it provides. A project manager who can show leadership a current SPI and CPI with trend data has a far more credible performance story than one who is relying on subjective status reports.`,

  'c2c1374a-73a5-4cca-b0d6-055af182c7cf': `Lesson 3.5 — AI-Powered Scheduling and Forecasting. Schedule management has traditionally been a highly manual discipline — creating the network diagram, calculating the critical path, updating progress, and reforecasting completion required significant PM time and expertise. AI is automating much of this work while simultaneously improving the quality of the analysis. Let me walk you through the most impactful AI applications in schedule management. Automated schedule generation uses AI to build an initial project schedule from a project description, WBS, and historical project data. Rather than building the network diagram manually, the AI drafts it based on typical activity sequences for this type of project and presents it to the PM for review and adjustment. This does not replace PM judgment — it creates a starting point that reduces schedule creation time by 50 to 70 percent. Predictive schedule risk analysis uses machine learning models trained on thousands of historical projects to identify which activities in your current schedule are at highest risk of delay. These predictions are based on factors like activity complexity, resource assignments, dependency chains, and historical performance of similar activities. Early warning enables proactive risk management rather than reactive crisis management. Dynamic schedule updating integrates with your project management tool and automatically updates the schedule based on task completion reports and time tracking data. Rather than manually updating the schedule after every standup, the AI processes completion updates and immediately shows the impact on critical path and projected completion date. For the PMP exam, you need to understand traditional scheduling methods deeply — the AI tools are built on top of these foundations. Understanding CPM, resource leveling, and schedule compression techniques is required both for the exam and for evaluating the quality of AI-generated schedule recommendations.`,

  // MODULE 4 - COST
  '47cb2cc0-eb60-4c32-9fe2-9edfa43a2d3b': `Welcome to Module 4, Lesson 4.1 — Cost Estimation Techniques. Cost estimation is the process of developing an approximation of the monetary resources needed to complete project work. Accurate cost estimation is one of the most valuable skills a project manager can develop — it is the foundation of stakeholder trust, project financial health, and organizational budgeting discipline. Let me walk you through the key estimation techniques and when to use each one. Order of magnitude estimates, sometimes called ROM estimates, are rough approximations made early in the project when detailed information is not available. ROM estimates typically have a range of negative 25 to positive 75 percent accuracy. They are appropriate for initial feasibility assessments and early budget conversations, but they should never be treated as commitments. Budget estimates have a range of negative 10 to plus 25 percent and are developed once the project scope is roughly defined but before detailed planning is complete. They serve as the basis for budget requests and organizational resource planning. Definitive estimates have a range of negative 5 to plus 10 percent and require a detailed WBS, resource plan, and schedule. They are developed during detailed planning and become the basis for the cost baseline. Analogous estimating uses actual cost data from previous similar projects. It is fast but less accurate than other methods. Its accuracy depends heavily on how comparable the reference projects actually are. Parametric estimating uses statistical relationships, such as cost per square foot in construction or cost per function point in software development. It is more accurate than analogous estimating when the parametric relationship is well-established. Bottom-up estimating decomposes the project to the individual activity level, estimates each activity independently, and aggregates to the project total. It is the most accurate technique and requires the most time to execute. For the PMP exam, you need to know all of these techniques, understand when each is appropriate, and be able to calculate key cost metrics.`,

  '9d8b4ef9-2391-4a83-b90a-754559867c80': `Lesson 4.2 — Budget Development and Cost Baseline. The project budget is not simply the sum of activity cost estimates. It includes the cost baseline, which is the approved time-phased budget used to measure, monitor, and control project cost performance, plus the management reserve, which is an amount held outside the cost baseline for unplanned changes in scope or cost. Understanding the distinction between these components is important for both financial management and PMP exam success. The budget development process starts with activity cost estimates and aggregates them upward through the WBS to work packages, control accounts, and ultimately the total project budget. This aggregation process reveals the total cost of delivering each deliverable and the total project cost. Contingency reserves are included within the cost baseline. They are planned amounts included to address identified risks — risks that have been analyzed and have a quantified probability and impact. Contingency reserves are owned by the project manager and can be accessed without management approval when the identified risk occurs. Management reserves, by contrast, are held outside the cost baseline for unknown unknowns — risks that have not been identified and therefore cannot be planned for. Accessing management reserves typically requires management approval because they represent a change to the cost baseline. The cost baseline is time-phased — it shows the planned spending profile over the life of the project, not just the total amount. This time-phasing is essential for cash flow planning and for calculating earned value metrics accurately. When the actual spending pattern deviates from the planned spending pattern, it signals a potential cost or schedule problem that requires investigation.`,

  'f7d2aadd-da49-4147-a5a0-8f11b5d8812f': `Lesson 4.3 — Cost Control and Earned Value Management. Cost control is the process of monitoring project costs, updating the cost baseline when changes are approved, and taking action to keep cost overruns within acceptable limits. It is one of the most technically demanding aspects of project management and a significant focus of the PMP exam. The foundation of cost control is the Earned Value Management system we introduced in the schedule module. Let me extend that discussion to cover the cost control applications of EVM that PMs use in practice. The Cost Performance Index, or CPI, is your most important indicator of cost efficiency. A CPI of 0.90 means you are getting 90 cents of value for every dollar spent — you are over budget. A CPI of 1.10 means you are getting 1.10 dollars of value for every dollar spent — you are under budget. The Estimate at Completion, or EAC, forecasts the total project cost at completion based on current performance. The most conservative formula is EAC equals Budget at Completion divided by CPI. This assumes your current cost performance will continue throughout the project. If you have a CPI of 0.85 and a BAC of one million dollars, your EAC is approximately 1.18 million. That is the number you bring to your sponsor. The Estimate to Complete, or ETC, is the expected cost needed to complete the remaining project work. ETC equals EAC minus AC. The Variance at Completion, or VAC, forecasts the final cost variance at project completion. VAC equals BAC minus EAC. A negative VAC means the project is expected to finish over budget. When your EVM data shows cost overruns, the appropriate response depends on the root cause. If the overrun is caused by scope additions, raise a change request. If it is caused by productivity problems, investigate root causes and develop recovery plans. If it is caused by estimation errors, develop revised estimates and communicate them proactively to stakeholders.`,

  '2997dd78-2be5-4ed2-ae51-041f0e6652d7': `Lesson 4.4 — Financial Justification and Return on Investment. Project managers who understand the business case and financial justification for their projects make better decisions and have more credible conversations with executive stakeholders. The financial analysis that justifies project investment uses several measures that you need to understand both for the PMP exam and for practical use. Net Present Value, or NPV, calculates the present value of all future cash flows from the project, discounted at the organization's cost of capital, minus the initial investment. A positive NPV means the project returns more than the cost of capital and creates shareholder value. A negative NPV means the project destroys value. When choosing between projects with limited budget, organizations should select the project with the highest positive NPV. Internal Rate of Return, or IRR, is the discount rate at which the NPV of the project equals zero — the effective return rate of the investment. Projects with an IRR above the organization's cost of capital create value. IRR is useful for comparing projects of different sizes. Payback period is the simplest measure — how long until the cumulative benefits exceed the initial investment. Organizations with tight cash flow constraints often use payback period as a filter: only projects with a payback period under a certain threshold are considered. Benefit-Cost Ratio, or BCR, divides the present value of benefits by the present value of costs. A BCR greater than one means benefits exceed costs. BCR is useful for quick comparisons but less rigorous than NPV for detailed financial analysis. As a project manager, you typically do not build the business case yourself — that is the sponsor's responsibility. But you need to understand these measures so you can participate in ROI conversations intelligently, make trade-off decisions that protect project value, and communicate project performance in terms that matter to the financial decision-makers in your organization.`,

  '7422ce76-ea9e-49df-9bf6-abf732d27c7e': `Lesson 4.5 — AI for Cost Management and Budget Forecasting. Cost overruns remain one of the most common project failures. Research consistently shows that large projects on average finish 80 percent over budget. AI is beginning to address this problem in meaningful ways — not by eliminating the complexity that causes cost overruns, but by giving project managers better information faster so they can intervene before small variances become large problems. AI-powered cost forecasting uses machine learning models trained on historical project cost data to predict future cost performance based on current trends. Unlike simple EVM extrapolation, AI models can identify non-linear cost patterns — for example, cost overruns that accelerate in the final 20 percent of a project, which is a common historical pattern — and generate more accurate forecasts. Automated variance analysis identifies the root causes of cost variances by analyzing project data across multiple dimensions: scope changes, resource utilization patterns, vendor invoices, and schedule delays. Instead of spending hours manually correlating data across systems, the PM receives an AI-generated variance report that identifies the primary drivers of cost overruns and their estimated contribution. Vendor and contract cost monitoring uses AI to analyze vendor invoices against contract terms, flagging billing anomalies, potential overcharges, and contract terms approaching limits. For projects with significant procurement spend, this monitoring can identify savings opportunities that manual review misses. Budget scenario modeling lets PMs run multiple budget scenarios — what if we accelerate this deliverable, what if this vendor is delayed, what if we need to add a resource — and immediately see the projected cost impact. AI makes these scenarios fast enough to run during a stakeholder meeting, enabling real-time what-if analysis that previously required hours of spreadsheet work.`,

  // MODULE 5 - RISK
  '1cab2268-2715-4c74-9b0f-887bfe7c085a': `Welcome to Module 5, Lesson 5.1 — Risk Identification and the Risk Register. Risk management is perhaps the most intellectually demanding aspect of project management because it requires thinking systematically about what might go wrong before it does. Great project managers are not risk-averse — they are risk-aware. They identify risks early, analyze them objectively, and plan responses that protect the project without over-engineering the solution. Risk identification is the first step. A risk is an uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, has a positive or negative effect on project objectives. Note that definition carefully: risks include positive outcomes, called opportunities, as well as negative outcomes, called threats. Many project managers only think about threats. Ignoring opportunities means missing potential schedule accelerations, cost savings, and quality improvements. Risk identification techniques include brainstorming sessions with the project team and key stakeholders; the Delphi technique, which uses anonymous expert input to build consensus on risks without group dynamics distorting the results; SWOT analysis, which examines strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats; checklists based on historical project data from similar projects; and assumption and constraint analysis, which examines the assumptions underlying the project plan and identifies what happens if they prove false. The risk register is the primary artifact of risk management. For each identified risk, the register captures a risk description, the risk category, the probability of occurrence on a scale of your choosing, the impact on project objectives if it occurs, the overall risk score — probability times impact — the risk owner, and the planned response. The risk register is a living document that is reviewed and updated regularly throughout the project lifecycle. Risks that were low probability early in the project may increase in probability as you approach the phases where they could materialize.`,

  'a1b2b1be-b175-4475-a8d8-b2ef74c236dc': `Lesson 5.2 — Qualitative and Quantitative Risk Analysis. Risk analysis transforms the list of identified risks into prioritized, actionable information for decision-making. There are two levels of analysis: qualitative, which prioritizes risks quickly using probability and impact assessments; and quantitative, which models the combined effect of risks on project objectives using numerical techniques. Qualitative risk analysis is performed first and is a required activity on most projects. The probability and impact matrix is the primary tool. It places each risk on a grid with probability on one axis and impact on the other, creating a risk score that enables prioritization. High probability, high impact risks receive the most management attention. Low probability, low impact risks are typically accepted. The output is a prioritized list of risks ranked by their risk score. Quantitative risk analysis provides additional analytical depth for high-priority risks and is particularly valuable for large, complex projects where stakeholders need probabilistic estimates of project outcomes. Monte Carlo simulation is the most common quantitative technique. It runs thousands of simulated project scenarios by randomly sampling from probability distributions for key risk variables — activity durations, costs, resource availability — and produces a probability distribution of possible project outcomes. The output might show that there is a 70 percent probability the project finishes on time and a 90 percent probability it finishes within 10 percent of schedule. This type of output gives stakeholders a realistic, data-driven picture of risk rather than a single-point estimate that everyone knows is uncertain. Sensitivity analysis, often shown as a tornado diagram, identifies which risks have the greatest individual impact on project outcomes. The risks at the top of the tornado diagram are the ones that deserve the most risk management investment.`,

  'a925344a-58dd-42b0-9ced-1db0f535a6f2': `Lesson 5.3 — Risk Response Strategies. Identifying and analyzing risks is only valuable if you do something about them. Risk response planning develops options and actions to enhance opportunities and reduce threats to project objectives. The four strategies for threats are avoid, transfer, mitigate, and accept. Avoidance changes the project plan to eliminate the threat entirely or to protect project objectives from its impact. This might mean changing the project approach, extending the schedule, or reducing scope to avoid the risky element. Avoidance is the most powerful response but also the most expensive — it typically requires significant changes to the project. Transfer shifts the impact of the threat to a third party, along with ownership of the response. Insurance is the classic transfer mechanism. Fixed-price contracts transfer cost risk to the vendor. Transfer does not eliminate the risk — it changes who bears the consequences if it occurs. Mitigation reduces the probability or impact of a threat. Adding additional testing reduces the probability of defects reaching production. Building a prototype reduces the impact of design uncertainty. Mitigation is the most common response strategy for moderate risks. Acceptance acknowledges that a threat exists and consciously decides to proceed without taking specific action. Active acceptance involves establishing a contingency reserve — time, money, or resources — to address the risk if it occurs. Passive acceptance does nothing and simply addresses the risk if and when it materializes. For opportunities, the corresponding strategies are exploit, share, enhance, and accept. Exploiting opportunities means taking deliberate action to ensure they materialize — the opposite of avoidance. Sharing involves partnering with an entity better positioned to capture the opportunity. Enhancing increases the probability or positive impact of the opportunity. Accepting allows the opportunity to occur or not without deliberate action.`,

  '3c93d1fb-5dd8-4f04-a564-da0966ccfc5c': `Lesson 5.4 — Risk Monitoring and Control. Risk management does not end with risk response planning. Risks change throughout the project — their probability evolves, new risks emerge, planned responses prove ineffective, and watched risks either materialize or pass without incident. Risk monitoring and control is the ongoing process of tracking identified risks, monitoring residual risks, identifying new risks, executing risk responses, and evaluating risk process effectiveness. The risk register must be a living document. Review it at a minimum at the start of each sprint or project phase and update it based on what has changed. Risks that were rated high probability early in the project may decrease in probability as the relevant project phase completes successfully. New risks that were not initially identified must be added and analyzed. Risk triggers — indicators that a risk is about to materialize — should be defined for each significant risk and monitored actively. If a risk trigger fires, the risk response should be activated immediately without waiting for the risk event to fully materialize. The workaround is the response to an unplanned risk event — a risk that was either not identified or not addressed with a planned response. Workarounds are developed and implemented in real time and should be documented in the risk register for future project reference. Risk reporting keeps stakeholders informed of the current risk landscape and the status of risk responses. For executive stakeholders, a high-level risk dashboard showing the top five risks with their current status is typically more valuable than the full risk register. For the project team, detailed risk information with clear ownership and response actions is needed. Calibrate your risk reporting to your audience.`,

  '8921a386-8205-4391-9b9c-2dabbcbec128': `Lesson 5.5 — AI for Risk Management. Risk management has historically been limited by the human capacity to identify, analyze, and monitor risks across complex projects. Project managers can realistically maintain active awareness of 10 to 20 risks simultaneously. AI dramatically expands this capacity, enabling continuous monitoring of hundreds of risk signals and pattern recognition across data sources that no human could synthesize manually. AI-powered risk identification analyzes project documentation, historical project data, industry databases, and external news sources to identify risks that the project team may not have considered. For example, an AI system monitoring a technology project might flag a new security vulnerability disclosure in a dependency library as a relevant risk, or identify a supplier financial distress report as a supply chain risk for a hardware project. These external signal risks are frequently the ones that blindside project teams. Predictive risk analytics uses machine learning models trained on historical project outcomes to predict which currently low-severity risks are most likely to escalate. These predictions are based on patterns in how similar risks have evolved in comparable past projects. Early warning on escalating risks enables proactive response before the risk becomes a crisis. Automated risk monitoring continuously analyzes project data — schedule performance, budget performance, team velocity, stakeholder communication patterns — and alerts the PM when combinations of indicators suggest increasing risk exposure. A single metric out of range is noise; a correlated pattern across multiple metrics is a signal that deserves immediate attention. For the PMP exam, you need to master the traditional risk management framework thoroughly. AI tools are built on top of these foundations, and the exam tests your understanding of the underlying principles.`,

  // MODULE 6 - STAKEHOLDER & COMMUNICATION
  'ef7080f5-37ee-4db5-a887-fc0441a2174f': `Welcome to Module 6, Lesson 6.1 — Stakeholder Engagement Strategies. Effective stakeholder engagement is the difference between projects that succeed despite obstacles and projects that fail despite good planning. Stakeholders who are engaged, informed, and appropriately involved in decision-making become project advocates. Stakeholders who are ignored, surprised, or left out of decisions they expected to influence become project adversaries. The engagement strategies available to you depend on the stakeholder's power-interest profile and their current attitude toward the project. For high-power, high-interest stakeholders, close management means regular one-on-one meetings, early involvement in key decisions, and transparency about project challenges before they become visible to others. These stakeholders should never be surprised by bad news — they should hear it from you first. For high-power, low-interest stakeholders, the goal is to maintain satisfaction without over-engaging. These stakeholders typically want summary-level information delivered at predictable intervals. They do not want to be buried in details, but they do want to feel confident that the project is under control. For low-power, high-interest stakeholders, regular information provision and genuine listening creates advocates who will use their informal influence networks to support the project. These stakeholders often have valuable operational knowledge that improves project decisions if you take the time to involve them appropriately. The stakeholder engagement assessment matrix tracks each stakeholder's current engagement level — unaware, resistant, neutral, supportive, or leading — against the desired engagement level. The gaps between current and desired levels drive your engagement action planning. Revisit this matrix monthly and update it as stakeholder attitudes evolve.`,

  '7663d302-711e-40e5-ac78-cab01dc79adb': `Lesson 6.2 — Communication Planning and Management. Projects fail for many reasons, but poor communication is among the most common. Stakeholders who do not understand what is happening, why decisions are being made, or what is expected of them create friction, make poor decisions, and lose confidence in the project team. A well-designed communication plan prevents these failures by ensuring the right information reaches the right people through the right channels at the right frequency. The communications management plan answers five questions for each piece of project communication. Who is the audience? What information do they need? When do they need it — what is the frequency and timing? How should it be delivered — meeting, email, dashboard, report? Who is responsible for producing and distributing it? The communication matrix documents these answers in a structured format that can be reviewed, approved by stakeholders, and used as the ongoing reference for communication activities. Common communication types in project management include status reports for executive and steering committee audiences, typically weekly or biweekly; team communication including standups, sprint ceremonies, and informal channels; stakeholder newsletters for broader project communities; escalation communications for issues requiring executive attention; and change notifications for approved scope, schedule, or budget changes. Communication channel selection matters. Formal communications — project status, change requests, escalations — should be documented in writing even if they are initially delivered verbally. Never rely solely on verbal communication for anything that might later be disputed. Push versus pull communication is an important design choice. Push communication sends information to the audience proactively — email, newsletter, meeting. Pull communication makes information available for stakeholders to access when they want it — project dashboard, document repository. For senior executives who are pressed for time, a pull model with automated alerts when metrics change is often more effective than regular push reports they may not have time to read.`,

  '45c179dd-56a8-4444-98b0-1a830f22cb09': `Lesson 6.3 — Conflict Management and Negotiation. Conflict is inevitable on projects. Resources are scarce, priorities compete, stakeholders have different needs, and team members have different working styles and values. The project manager who tries to avoid conflict entirely creates a culture where important tensions never surface — and those unaddressed tensions eventually derail the project. The project manager who manages conflict skillfully creates a team where disagreements are resolved productively and relationships are strengthened rather than damaged. There are five conflict resolution styles, ranging from most to least confrontational. Collaborating, also called problem solving, seeks a solution that fully satisfies all parties. It is the most time-consuming but produces the most durable resolutions. It is appropriate when relationships are important and the issue is significant enough to warrant the investment. Compromising finds a middle ground that partially satisfies all parties. It is faster than collaborating but leaves everyone partially unsatisfied. Appropriate when a good-enough solution is preferable to delay. Accommodating yields to the other party's position to preserve the relationship. Appropriate when the issue is more important to them than to you, or when you realize you are wrong. Forcing imposes your position on the other party. Fast and effective when you have the authority and the urgency demands immediate resolution, but it damages relationships and resentment tends to surface later. Avoiding withdraws from the conflict without resolution. Appropriate only as a temporary measure when emotions are running high and immediate resolution is likely to make things worse. Negotiation is a specific form of conflict resolution that applies when you need to reach agreement with another party on terms, resources, or decisions. Principled negotiation separates the people from the problem, focuses on interests rather than positions, generates multiple options before settling on a solution, and insists on objective criteria for evaluating options.`,

  'ebd2b187-46f4-4d89-8117-5edb28b29a40': `Lesson 6.4 — Virtual Team Management. The shift to remote and hybrid work has made virtual team management one of the most critical competencies for project managers. Managing distributed teams presents specific challenges that do not exist or are much less significant in co-located environments: communication latency, cultural and time zone differences, reduced informal relationship-building opportunities, and the difficulty of reading team dynamics without physical presence cues. Virtual team success starts with explicit communication of expectations that would be implicit in a co-located environment. Response time expectations — when someone sends a message, how quickly should they expect a reply? Working hours overlap — when are all team members expected to be available simultaneously? Meeting cadence — how frequently does the whole team meet synchronously versus communicating asynchronously? Documentation standards — what needs to be written down versus handled through informal conversation? Intentional relationship building is essential in virtual environments. Relationships that form naturally through shared physical space in offices — through hallway conversations, lunch breaks, and casual encounters — must be deliberately created in virtual environments. Virtual team building activities, informal video calls without work agenda, and shared digital spaces for non-work communication all contribute to the social fabric that makes distributed teams cohesive. Asynchronous collaboration tools are the backbone of effective virtual team management. Notion or Confluence for documentation and knowledge sharing, Slack or Teams for real-time communication, Loom for video messages that avoid synchronous meeting overhead, and shared project dashboards that give everyone visibility into project status without requiring a meeting to distribute information. For project managers, virtual team management also requires more explicit conflict detection — you will not walk past two team members who are visibly uncomfortable with each other. You need to build explicit check-in processes that surface interpersonal issues before they affect team performance.`,

  '825743e8-e90a-4599-b807-55236eabc023': `Lesson 6.5 — AI for Stakeholder and Communication Management. Communication and stakeholder management have historically been entirely human-driven activities — the quality of your relationships, your listening skills, and your communication judgment determined your effectiveness. AI is now augmenting these capabilities in ways that improve both the efficiency and quality of stakeholder engagement. AI-powered communication drafting reduces the time spent on routine communication while maintaining quality. Status reports, meeting summaries, stakeholder notifications, and change request communications can all be drafted by AI in seconds and reviewed by the PM in minutes. The output quality, especially when you have provided good context and used well-crafted prompts, is typically professional and appropriate for the audience. Sentiment analysis applied to project communications — emails, meeting transcripts, survey responses — can identify stakeholder concerns, frustrations, and attitude shifts that might not be explicitly stated. When a key stakeholder's email tone shifts from enthusiastic to neutral to guarded over three weeks, AI can flag this pattern and alert you to investigate before the relationship deteriorates further. Stakeholder communication personalization becomes more scalable with AI assistance. Different stakeholders need different information in different formats. AI can take a single project status dataset and generate five different stakeholder-specific communication versions — executive summary, technical briefing, operational impact summary, customer-facing status, and team update — in the time it would take you to write one. Meeting preparation and follow-up automation handles the routine aspects of meeting management: generating agenda drafts from project context, summarizing decisions and action items after meetings, and tracking action item completion across stakeholder communications. This frees PM time for the genuinely relationship-driven aspects of stakeholder management that require human judgment and empathy.`,

  // MODULE 7 - QUALITY
  'fb9360fe-22c2-4560-bdab-672169d65e26': `Welcome to Module 7, Lesson 7.1 — Quality Planning and Standards. Quality management in project management answers a fundamental question: how do we ensure that the project deliverables meet the standards that stakeholders expect? The answer requires planning, assurance, and control activities that run throughout the project lifecycle. Quality planning is the process of identifying quality requirements and standards for the project and its deliverables, and documenting how the project will demonstrate compliance. The quality management plan specifies what quality standards apply, how quality will be measured, what quality metrics will be tracked, and what quality tools and techniques will be used. Quality standards come from multiple sources: organizational standards and policies, industry standards such as ISO 9000, regulatory requirements, customer contractual requirements, and the project-specific standards established in the quality management plan. One of the most important distinctions in quality management is between the cost of conformance and the cost of non-conformance. The cost of conformance includes all money spent during the project to prevent failures: training, process documentation, inspection, testing, and quality assurance activities. The cost of non-conformance includes all money spent during and after the project because of failures: rework, warranty work, liability, and lost business. Research consistently shows that the cost of non-conformance is three to five times higher than the cost of conformance. Investing in quality upfront is dramatically more economical than fixing quality failures after the fact. Quality metrics are specific measurements used to determine whether quality requirements are being met. Defect density — defects per unit of deliverable — is a common quality metric in software projects. On-time delivery rate, customer satisfaction scores, and first-pass yield are quality metrics in manufacturing and service delivery contexts.`,

  'cf1584db-c29c-41b8-a8af-f39a4afaf4ad': `Lesson 7.2 — Quality Assurance and Process Improvement. Quality assurance is the process of auditing quality requirements and quality control measurements to ensure appropriate quality standards and operational definitions are used. It is focused on the processes used to create deliverables, not the deliverables themselves. The distinction between quality assurance and quality control is important: QA is process-focused and preventive; quality control is product-focused and detective. Quality audits are formal examinations of project activities to determine whether they comply with organizational and project policies, processes, and procedures. Audits identify gaps between how work is being done and how it should be done according to the quality management plan. A well-designed audit does not blame individuals — it identifies process weaknesses that enable individuals to make errors or produce inconsistent work. Process improvement is a continuous activity in quality management. The Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle, or PDCA, provides a simple framework for systematic process improvement. Plan identifies the process improvement opportunity and designs the change. Do implements the change on a small scale. Check evaluates the results of the change against the expected improvement. Act standardizes the improvement if it worked, or redesigns and re-tries if it did not. Lean principles and Six Sigma methodologies provide more sophisticated frameworks for process improvement. Lean focuses on eliminating waste — activities that consume resources but do not add value from the customer's perspective. Six Sigma focuses on reducing process variation to near-zero defect rates using statistical analysis. The DMAIC process — Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control — is the Six Sigma improvement methodology that project managers encounter most frequently in enterprise environments.`,

  '6cdd439f-1cb1-4f5a-9591-4b7a3fca6460': `Lesson 7.3 — Quality Control Tools and Metrics. Quality control is the process of monitoring and recording results of executing quality activities to assess performance and recommend necessary changes. It is the detective function of quality management — catching defects before they reach the customer. The seven basic quality tools are the foundational quality control instruments that every project manager should be proficient with. The cause-and-effect diagram, also called a fishbone or Ishikawa diagram, systematically identifies potential causes of a quality problem by categorizing causes into major categories — typically people, processes, equipment, materials, environment, and management. This structured analysis prevents the common mistake of jumping to solutions before the root cause is understood. The control chart monitors process performance over time, showing upper and lower control limits based on the process's natural variation. When data points fall outside the control limits or show non-random patterns within the limits, it signals that the process is out of control — something has changed that requires investigation. Flow charts document the process or system being analyzed, making it easier to identify where quality failures occur and what causes them. Check sheets are structured forms for collecting and analyzing data. They are simple but effective for tracking defect frequency, location, and type. Histograms display the distribution of data and reveal whether the process is performing within specification limits and how data is distributed. Pareto charts combine a bar chart of defect counts by category with a cumulative line, visually identifying the vital few causes that account for the majority of quality problems. The Pareto principle — that 80 percent of problems come from 20 percent of causes — guides quality improvement efforts toward the highest-leverage interventions.`,

  'aa458bf9-c829-4977-a47a-88d94cbad862': `Lesson 7.4 — Agile Quality Practices. Quality management in agile environments operates on different principles than traditional project quality management. Rather than planning quality upfront and inspecting for it at the end, agile teams build quality in throughout the development process through practices that prevent defects rather than detecting them. Test-Driven Development, or TDD, reverses the traditional development sequence. Instead of writing code and then writing tests, developers write failing tests first, then write the minimum code to make the tests pass. This practice produces code with high test coverage by definition, forces developers to think about edge cases before writing implementation, and creates a comprehensive regression test suite that catches defects immediately when code changes break existing functionality. Continuous Integration, or CI, merges developers' code changes into a shared repository frequently — multiple times per day — and automatically runs the test suite on every merge. This practice catches integration conflicts and test failures within minutes of their introduction rather than discovering them days or weeks later during integration testing. Pair programming has two developers working together on the same code simultaneously — one writing code and one reviewing in real time. This practice produces higher quality code than solo development and builds team knowledge through continuous peer learning. Definition of Done is the agile quality checkpoint. Before any story can be considered complete, it must meet all criteria in the Definition of Done — which typically includes code written and reviewed, unit tests written and passing, integration tests passing, documentation updated, and product owner acceptance received. The Definition of Done is the team's quality standard, not just a task checklist.`,

  'd1385f54-cf31-461b-a8d5-5f8ccf88249e': `Lesson 7.5 — AI for Quality Management. Artificial intelligence is transforming quality management from a sampling-based discipline into a continuous, comprehensive monitoring capability. Traditional quality control relies on statistical sampling — inspecting a percentage of output to infer the quality of the whole. AI enables inspection of 100 percent of output continuously, catching quality issues that sampling would miss. AI-powered defect detection in manufacturing uses computer vision to inspect every product at production speed, identifying defects that human inspectors miss due to fatigue, attention variability, and the limitations of human visual processing. Defect detection rates with AI vision systems typically exceed human inspector accuracy by 20 to 40 percent while processing at machine speed. In software development, AI code analysis tools scan 100 percent of committed code for security vulnerabilities, performance issues, and code quality violations — providing immediate feedback to developers rather than waiting for formal code review. This shifts defect detection earlier in the development process, dramatically reducing the cost of fixing defects. AI-assisted test generation creates comprehensive test cases from requirements specifications, covering edge cases and combinations that human test writers typically miss. The coverage improvement reduces the defect escape rate — defects that pass through testing and reach customers — which is typically the most costly quality failure mode. Process quality monitoring uses AI to analyze process execution data in real time, detecting deviations from standard operating procedures before they produce defective outputs. In regulated industries like pharmaceuticals and medical devices, where process compliance is a legal requirement, AI monitoring provides continuous compliance assurance rather than periodic audits.`,

  // MODULE 8 - PROCUREMENT
  '83257377-4aec-4047-b170-8f6de01518a7': `Welcome to Module 8, Lesson 8.1 — Procurement Planning. Procurement management is the process of purchasing or acquiring products, services, or results from outside the project team. Effective procurement management can significantly accelerate delivery, access specialized capabilities, and shift risk to parties better positioned to manage it. Poor procurement management creates cost overruns, schedule delays, quality problems, and legal disputes. Procurement planning starts with the make-or-buy decision: for each component of the project, should we produce it with internal resources or procure it externally? This decision considers cost, capability, timeline, risk, and strategic importance. Components that are not core competencies, require specialized expertise not available internally, or need to be delivered in parallel with other work are candidates for procurement. The procurement management plan documents the decisions made during procurement planning: what will be procured, through what contract type, using what procurement process, and with what timelines. It also specifies the roles and responsibilities for procurement activities, the pre-qualified vendor list if one exists, and the criteria for evaluating vendor proposals. Statement of Work, or SOW, is the document that describes the work to be performed under a contract. A well-written SOW is specific enough to enable accurate vendor pricing and unambiguous enough to avoid disputes about what was and was not included in the contract scope. Vague SOWs are one of the most common root causes of procurement disputes. The SOW should describe the work to be performed, the deliverables to be produced, the performance standards required, and the timeline for completion.`,

  '022742f9-7eef-4785-b291-1fde736da89a': `Lesson 8.2 — Contract Types and Risk Allocation. The choice of contract type is one of the most significant risk management decisions in procurement management. Different contract types allocate cost risk between the buyer and the seller in fundamentally different ways, and choosing the wrong contract type for the situation can expose your project to significant cost overruns or limit your ability to get good value from vendors. Fixed-price contracts set a fixed total price for a defined scope of work. The seller accepts all cost risk — if it costs them more than the contract price to deliver, they absorb the loss. The buyer's risk is quality and scope risk — a financially stressed vendor may cut corners to maintain margin. Fixed-price contracts are appropriate when the scope is well-defined and stable, the seller can accurately estimate the cost, and neither party expects significant change. Cost-reimbursable contracts reimburse the seller for allowable costs and add a fee. The buyer accepts most cost risk because all legitimate costs are reimbursed regardless of the total. Cost-plus-fixed-fee adds a fixed fee regardless of actual costs, giving the seller no incentive to control costs. Cost-plus-incentive-fee adds an incentive fee when performance targets are met, providing cost control motivation. Cost-reimbursable contracts are appropriate when scope is uncertain, the work is innovative, or the buyer wants to maintain close control over how the work is performed. Time-and-materials contracts pay for time at agreed rates and reimburse material costs. They are a hybrid that is appropriate for staff augmentation, consulting, and situations where scope is unclear but the buyer wants to limit the seller's margin. T&M contracts require close management because costs accumulate based on time spent, creating seller incentive to work slowly.`,

  'a5132d9d-88e4-4fe6-b50a-49d4e115df6b': `Lesson 8.3 — Vendor Selection and Negotiation. Vendor selection is a process that requires both analytical rigor and relationship judgment. The best vendor on paper is not always the best vendor in practice — culture fit, communication responsiveness, and the specific team that will work on your project matter as much as the formal evaluation criteria. Source selection criteria should be established before issuing the Request for Proposal or Request for Quotation. Establishing criteria after receiving proposals creates bias risk and procurement integrity problems. Common evaluation criteria include technical approach and methodology, relevant experience and references, management approach, price, financial stability, and compliance with contractual requirements. The weighting of these criteria should reflect the project's priorities — a complex, high-risk project might weight technical approach and experience more heavily than price, while a commodity procurement might weight price most heavily. Proposal evaluation typically involves both a technical evaluation and a commercial evaluation. The technical evaluation assesses the vendor's approach, experience, and proposed team against the technical requirements. The commercial evaluation assesses price, payment terms, and contract conditions. Keeping these evaluations separate until the technical evaluation is complete prevents price from unduly influencing technical assessment. Contract negotiation seeks to reach mutually acceptable terms that protect the project's interests while enabling the vendor to perform profitably. Adversarial negotiation that leaves the vendor feeling exploited typically results in a vendor who is minimally compliant and difficult to work with. Principled negotiation that recognizes both parties' legitimate interests produces contracts that both parties are motivated to perform well under.`,

  'c299e851-0c0e-43cd-ad1f-74ec123eff66': `Lesson 8.4 — Vendor Performance Management. Awarding a contract is the beginning of vendor management, not the end. The vendor's performance during contract execution determines whether the procurement delivers its intended value. Many project managers under-invest in vendor performance management, treating contracts as self-executing once signed. This is a mistake — vendors require clear expectations, consistent monitoring, and constructive feedback to consistently deliver contract performance. Performance monitoring should be specified in the contract itself: what will be measured, how frequently, what thresholds trigger formal review, and what remedies apply to performance deficiencies. Monthly performance reviews with formal scorecards create accountability without micromanagement. The scorecard should include quality metrics, schedule adherence, communication responsiveness, and any project-specific performance indicators. Relationship management is as important as performance monitoring. The vendor team has competing priorities across multiple clients. Vendors who feel valued and well-managed by a client tend to prioritize that client when resources are constrained. Regular check-ins beyond the formal performance review, acknowledgment of good performance, and fair handling of disputes build the relationship capital that pays dividends when problems arise. Change management in vendor relationships requires careful attention to contract scope. Every change to the vendor's scope must go through a formal change order process that adjusts the contract price and timeline accordingly. Verbal agreements to scope changes that are not documented in contract amendments create disputes that are expensive and damaging to relationships. When vendor performance is deficient, escalation should follow a clear path: informal discussion, formal notice, escalation to the vendor's management, and ultimately contract remedies if performance does not improve. Document every step of this process in writing.`,

  'eca9c0e4-e9ef-400e-8fe0-acbaea1badb5': `Lesson 8.5 — AI for Procurement Management. Procurement management is a data-intensive discipline — vendor research, proposal evaluation, contract analysis, and performance monitoring all involve processing large volumes of information to support decisions. AI is dramatically improving the efficiency and quality of these processes. AI-powered vendor research and qualification analyzes thousands of potential vendors against your qualification criteria in minutes, surfacing the best-fit options from a landscape that would take days to research manually. By analyzing vendor financial health, project portfolio, client references, and capability data from multiple sources, AI creates a vendor shortlist with supporting evidence that enables informed procurement decisions. Contract analysis and risk identification uses AI to review contract terms and flag clauses that deviate from standard terms, represent unusual risk allocation, or contain ambiguities that could lead to disputes. Legal teams have used AI for contract review for several years, and this capability is now accessible to procurement practitioners without legal backgrounds through commercial tools. For large contract libraries, AI can also identify obligations, rights, expiration dates, and performance requirements across hundreds of contracts simultaneously — a task that previously required significant legal and administrative resources. Vendor performance analytics combines multiple data streams — invoice data, delivery records, quality metrics, communication logs — to generate continuous vendor performance scores that identify issues before they become contract disputes. AI pattern recognition can identify early warning signals in vendor behavior — such as decreasing responsiveness or increasing change order frequency — that predict future performance problems. Procurement spend analysis uses AI to analyze organizational purchasing patterns and identify consolidation opportunities, preferred vendor compliance rates, and contract savings opportunities. For organizations with decentralized procurement, AI spend analysis often identifies 10 to 20 percent cost reduction opportunities from vendor consolidation alone.`,

  // MODULE 9 - AGILE
  '6bc29b2a-b141-486d-9f80-a31674c3aa51': `Welcome to Module 9, Lesson 9.1 — Agile Manifesto and Principles for Project Managers. The Agile Manifesto, published in 2001 by seventeen software practitioners, is the foundation document of the modern agile movement. Its four value statements and twelve principles have shaped how millions of projects are managed globally. Every PMP candidate needs to understand these values and principles deeply — not just to pass the exam, but because the PMP exam now reflects the reality that most project managers work in hybrid environments where agile and traditional approaches must coexist. The four values state that agile practitioners value individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over comprehensive documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a plan. The second element in each pair has value — processes, documentation, contracts, and plans are all important. The agile assertion is that the first element in each pair has more value in the contexts where agile was designed to apply. The twelve principles elaborate on these values. They include delivering working software frequently, welcoming changing requirements even late in development, building projects around motivated individuals, promoting sustainable development pace, technical excellence and good design as foundations of agility, and simplicity — maximizing the amount of work not done — as essential. For project managers transitioning to agile or hybrid environments, the most important mindset shift is from planning and control to collaboration and adaptation. Traditional PM uses planning and control to manage risk. Agile uses frequent delivery and customer feedback to manage risk. Both are valid risk management approaches — they reflect different assumptions about the nature of the work and the stability of requirements.`,

  '0552f855-bc2c-428c-8342-9ffe8778b0ea': `Lesson 9.2 — Scrum for Project Managers. Scrum is the most widely adopted agile framework globally, and it is the framework most project managers encounter when their organizations adopt agile practices. For PMs with traditional backgrounds, Scrum presents both opportunity and challenge. The opportunity is a framework that is genuinely effective for complex, fast-changing work. The challenge is that Scrum deliberately eliminates several traditional PM functions — the PM does not write the project plan, does not assign tasks to team members, and does not serve as the primary point of accountability for delivery. The three Scrum roles are the Product Owner, who maximizes product value by managing and prioritizing the product backlog; the Development Team, who self-organizes to deliver potentially shippable product increments each sprint; and the Scrum Master, who facilitates the Scrum process, removes impediments, and coaches the team and organization on Scrum principles. There is no Project Manager role in Scrum. Traditional PMs who transition to Scrum typically take on either the Product Owner role, focusing on requirements and prioritization, or the Scrum Master role, focusing on process facilitation and impediment removal. Some become Agile Program Managers, coordinating across multiple Scrum teams. The five Scrum events are the Sprint, a time-boxed period of one to four weeks during which a done product increment is created; Sprint Planning, which establishes the Sprint Goal and Sprint Backlog; the Daily Scrum, a fifteen-minute daily synchronization for the Development Team; the Sprint Review, where the team demonstrates the increment to stakeholders and collects feedback; and the Sprint Retrospective, where the team inspects and adapts their process. The three Scrum artifacts are the Product Backlog, the Sprint Backlog, and the Product Increment.`,

  '3477be00-a3e6-4889-a354-fa7298117b5e': `Lesson 9.3 — Kanban and Lean for Project Managers. While Scrum is the most widely adopted agile framework, Kanban and Lean principles offer complementary approaches that are particularly valuable for operational work, support teams, and organizations that cannot adopt fixed-length sprints. Understanding these approaches expands your agile toolkit and helps you select the right framework for different types of work. Kanban is a method for managing work based on visualizing workflow, limiting work in progress, and managing flow. The Kanban board visualizes all work items as cards moving through workflow stages — typically To Do, In Progress, and Done, though more complex workflows can have many more stages. The critical practice that distinguishes Kanban from a simple task board is WIP limits — explicit limits on the number of items that can be in any given workflow stage simultaneously. WIP limits prevent the common dysfunction of teams taking on more work than they can complete, which causes context switching, longer cycle times, and poor quality. When a workflow stage is at its WIP limit and someone wants to add a new item, the team must first complete work in that stage before pulling new work. This constraint focuses attention and reduces multitasking. Lean principles originate from the Toyota Production System and focus on eliminating the eight types of waste: defects, overproduction, waiting, non-utilized talent, transportation of unnecessary materials, inventory, motion, and extra processing. For knowledge work, the most relevant waste types are defects (rework), waiting (blocked work), non-utilized talent (team members not fully engaged), inventory (work in progress that has not been delivered), and extra processing (unnecessary steps or documentation that does not add value). Lean and Kanban are often used together and are particularly well-suited to IT operations, customer support, marketing, and any team doing continuous operational work rather than time-bounded projects.`,

  '91588235-2f10-47f4-9fea-421e763c876d': `Lesson 9.4 — Hybrid Project Management. The reality of most enterprise projects is neither purely traditional nor purely agile. Most significant initiatives require some elements of upfront planning and fixed commitments alongside the flexibility and iterative delivery that agile provides. Hybrid project management combines the most appropriate elements of both approaches for the specific project context. The most common hybrid pattern is an agile execution engine within a traditional governance framework. The project has a traditional project charter, a high-level plan with milestones and budget, and formal change control for significant scope changes. But within the execution phase, work is organized in agile sprints with backlog refinement, sprint planning, and retrospectives. This approach gives executive stakeholders the predictability and control they expect while giving the delivery team the flexibility and feedback loops they need. Another common hybrid pattern is phased delivery within a traditional project structure, where each phase uses agile practices internally. Phase 1 delivers a minimum viable product, Phase 2 adds features based on user feedback, and Phase 3 finalizes the product. Each phase has fixed scope, budget, and timeline commitments, but execution within each phase is agile. Managing a hybrid project requires explicit agreement with stakeholders about which elements are fixed and which are flexible, how changes are handled differently in agile components versus traditional components, and how agile team velocity is translated into traditional project milestones and budget tracking. The biggest hybrid project management challenge is stakeholder expectations — executives often want the flexibility of agile to accommodate their changing priorities while maintaining the certainty of traditional project commitments on scope and timeline. Managing that tension requires ongoing stakeholder education and honest, data-driven communication about what is and is not possible.`,

  '62cf946e-b187-41a5-965e-c363ac071476': `Lesson 9.5 — Scaled Agile for Large Programs. Individual team agility is relatively straightforward to achieve. Scaling agile across dozens or hundreds of teams working on interdependent programs is one of the most challenging organizational problems in modern enterprise project management. Several scaling frameworks have emerged to address this challenge. The Scaled Agile Framework, or SAFe, is the most widely adopted scaling framework. It organizes work at three levels: team, program, and portfolio. At the team level, individual Scrum or Kanban teams work in standard sprints. At the program level, a group of teams — called an Agile Release Train or ART — work in synchronized Program Increments, typically eight to twelve weeks long. The ART includes all the teams needed to deliver a significant feature set, reducing cross-team dependencies. At the portfolio level, strategic themes, epic funding, and lean governance align team and program work to organizational strategy. SAFe introduces several roles that project managers often take on: the Release Train Engineer is the chief Scrum Master for the ART, facilitating cross-team ceremonies and removing ART-level impediments; the Product Manager manages the program backlog at the feature level; and the Business Owner represents business value and financial accountability for the ART. Program Increment Planning is SAFe's signature ceremony — a two-day event where all ART teams plan their work for the next 8-12 weeks together, identifying cross-team dependencies, risks, and commitments. The quality of PI Planning determines the quality of the ART's delivery for the entire PI. For the PMP exam, you need to understand SAFe at a conceptual level, know the key roles and ceremonies, and understand how agile scaling addresses the coordination challenges of large-program delivery.`,

  // MODULE 10 - LEADERSHIP
  '69675c5f-b5da-4fe4-8700-d1f8272e6cd9': `Welcome to Module 10, Lesson 10.1 — PM Leadership and Emotional Intelligence. Technical knowledge of project management methodologies is necessary but not sufficient for project success. The project managers who consistently deliver successful outcomes — especially on complex, multi-stakeholder projects — are distinguished by their leadership effectiveness. And the most important component of leadership effectiveness is emotional intelligence. Emotional intelligence, defined by Daniel Goleman, comprises four domains: self-awareness, the ability to recognize your own emotions and their impact on your behavior; self-management, the ability to regulate your emotions and maintain performance under pressure; social awareness, particularly empathy — the ability to understand the emotional states of others and their likely responses; and relationship management, the ability to influence, inspire, and build productive relationships. For project managers, emotional intelligence is not a soft skill — it is a core competency that directly affects every critical PM activity. Self-awareness helps you recognize when your own stress or frustration is affecting your team interactions negatively. Self-management enables you to deliver difficult news to stakeholders with composure rather than anxiety, and to navigate political conflicts without becoming reactive. Social awareness helps you read stakeholder concerns that are not being expressed explicitly, and recognize when team members need support before their performance is affected. Relationship management is the foundation of your ability to influence without authority — the primary leadership tool available to project managers in matrix and functional organizations. Developing emotional intelligence is a lifelong practice. Start with honest self-reflection about your typical emotional responses under pressure. Seek feedback from team members and peers about your leadership impact. Practice pausing before reacting in high-pressure situations. The investment in emotional intelligence development pays returns across every project and every relationship for the rest of your career.`,

  '2f72afe4-a68a-4011-9ea3-c5608ac8fca2': `Lesson 10.2 — Strategic PM and Business Acumen. The most effective project managers understand their projects not just as delivery challenges but as strategic investments. They understand why the project matters to the organization, how it connects to organizational strategy, and what business outcomes it is expected to produce. This strategic perspective shapes every significant decision they make during project execution. Business acumen for project managers means understanding the business context your project operates in: the industry dynamics, competitive landscape, customer needs, and organizational strategy that define why your project was initiated and what success looks like from a business perspective. A PM who understands this context makes better scope prioritization decisions — prioritizing features that accelerate competitive advantage over features that are technically interesting but strategically marginal. They make better resource allocation decisions — protecting resources for the work that most directly delivers business value. And they have more credible conversations with executive stakeholders because they speak the language of business outcomes rather than just project deliverables. Benefits realization management is an extension of strategic project management that tracks whether the business benefits projected in the business case are actually realized after the project closes. Many organizations deliver projects on time and on budget, then discover that the expected business benefits never materialized because the organizational changes needed to realize them were not made. A strategically-oriented PM ensures that benefits realization is planned, the organizational changes needed to realize benefits are scoped and resourced as part of the project, and accountability for benefits realization extends beyond project closure to the operational teams that will actually capture the value.`,

  '61485b8f-8857-43ec-bb96-9edf3f9a25ef': `Lesson 10.3 — PMP Certification Preparation. You have now covered the core knowledge areas and process groups tested on the PMP exam. In this lesson, I want to give you a practical preparation strategy that will help you pass the exam efficiently while also building the genuine expertise that makes the certification meaningful. The PMP exam has evolved significantly in recent years. The current exam reflects the reality that most PMs work in hybrid environments, testing both predictive and agile approaches with roughly equal weight. Approximately half the exam questions cover agile and hybrid approaches; the other half cover predictive project management. Both domains are represented throughout this course. The exam format is 180 questions over 230 minutes, with two ten-minute breaks. Questions include multiple choice, multiple response, drag-and-drop, and hotspot formats. The scoring is complex — not all questions are weighted equally — but the passing threshold is roughly 60 to 65 percent correct. The most effective preparation strategy combines conceptual understanding with scenario-based practice. The PMP exam tests application, not memorization. The most common mistake candidates make is spending too much time memorizing formulas and process names and not enough time practicing how to apply concepts to realistic project scenarios. Practice questions are essential. Complete at least 500 to 600 practice questions before the exam, reviewing the rationale for every question you got wrong. This analysis builds the scenario recognition patterns that the exam requires. Focus your final week of preparation on your weak areas rather than reviewing your strong areas. The exam rewards comprehensive competency, not deep expertise in a few areas. On exam day, read every question carefully before answering. Many questions are deliberately worded to make a wrong answer seem correct at first reading. Manage your time — you have approximately 77 seconds per question on average.`,

  '9fa1ba49-bd3f-4590-92e6-640be9ade23e': `Lesson 10.4 — Building Your PM Career. Earning the PMP certification is a significant career milestone, but it is the beginning of your PM career development journey, not the destination. Let me give you a concrete career development framework for the five years following certification. In years one and two, focus on depth over breadth. Take every opportunity to work on complex projects in your current organization. Seek out the difficult projects that others avoid — the ones with challenging stakeholders, unclear scope, or tight timelines. This is where you will develop the judgment that separates good project managers from great ones. Document your project outcomes, the challenges you navigated, and the results you delivered. Build your portfolio of project evidence. In years two and four, expand your context. Work in different industries, different organizational structures, or different types of projects. A PM who has only worked in software development benefits enormously from a construction or healthcare project management experience. Each domain adds tools to your toolkit and broadens your perspective on what project management can look like. Pursue specialized certifications that align with your career direction: SAFe certification for enterprise agile, PMI-ACP for agile project management, PgMP for program management, or PfMP for portfolio management. Develop your specialization. The highest-compensated project managers are not generalists — they are experts in a specific type of work, industry, or organizational context. The PMP gives you the foundation; your specialization gives you the differentiation. In years four and five, build your influence. Mentor junior project managers, speak at industry events, contribute to PMI chapter activities, and build your professional reputation. Your network and your reputation will be the primary drivers of your career advancement at this stage, supplemented by your continuing track record of delivery excellence.`,

  '96653a43-c09f-4bdb-8fc2-8f726adaf3f3': `Lesson 10.5 — Capstone: Your AI-Powered PM Operating System. We have arrived at the final lesson of the Project Management Professional course, and I want you to leave with something concrete: a personal PM Operating System that integrates everything you have learned into a practical, daily-use framework powered by the AI tools we have discussed throughout the course. Your PM Operating System has five layers. Layer one is your foundational methodology — the framework you use as your default approach. For most PMs, this is a hybrid of PMBOK-based predictive practices for governance and planning combined with agile practices for execution. Document your personal methodology framework: which processes you always use, which you use situationally, and which you have deliberately decided not to use. Layer two is your stakeholder management system — your approach to identifying, analyzing, and engaging stakeholders throughout the project lifecycle. This includes your stakeholder register template, your engagement strategy framework, and your communication planning approach. Layer three is your risk management discipline — the specific techniques you use for risk identification, analysis, and monitoring, and the tools and templates you have built to support these activities. Layer four is your AI-powered workflow — the specific AI tools you use for each major PM activity: status reporting, meeting documentation, risk analysis, scope management, and stakeholder communication. Document the prompts, templates, and workflows you have built through this course. Layer five is your continuous learning commitment — the books you will read, the communities you will participate in, the certifications you will pursue, and the mentoring relationships you will seek out. Project management is a discipline that rewards lifelong learning. The practitioners who stay curious and keep developing their skills consistently outperform those who stop learning after certification. Solo excelencia. Nothing but excellence. Congratulations on completing this course.`,
};

async function run() {
  const ids = Object.keys(transcripts);
  console.log(`Seeding ${ids.length} PM course lessons...`);
  for (const id of ids) {
    const { error } = await supabase
      .from('videos')
      .update({ translations: { en: { transcript: transcripts[id] } } })
      .eq('id', id);
    if (error) console.error('❌', id, error.message);
    else console.log('✅', id);
  }
  console.log('Done with batch 3 - PM Course complete.');
}
run();
